<div class="local-card"><div><b>Local claim risks</b><ul class="chips"><li>Ice &amp; snow</li><li>Mountain wind</li><li>Hail</li><li>Tree strikes</li><li>Frozen pipes</li></ul></div><div><b>Housing mix</b><p>Tree Streets homes, mountain-view builds, student rentals, older brick.</p></div></div>

<h2>Northeast Tennessee weather</h2>
<p>Johnson City sits in the valley between the Appalachian ridges, where winter storms can bring heavy snow and ice and strong mountain winds funnel through gaps. Spring and summer thunderstorms bring hail and damaging winds. In late September 2024, the remnants of Hurricane Helene caused catastrophic flooding in Unicoi County, Carter County and Washington County. Floodwater is covered by flood insurance rather than homeowner policies, but wind and tree damage from the same storm fall under standard property coverage.</p>
<h2>The Tree Streets and beyond</h2>
<p>Johnson City’s Tree Streets neighborhood is full of early 20th-century homes with deep porches, wood siding and mature trees overhead. East Tennessee State University brings student rentals and small apartment buildings. Newer homes in North Johnson City and the hills toward Boones Creek and Gray offer mountain views but also more exposure to wind and steep-roof charges.</p>
<h2>Common claims we see</h2>
<ul>
<li>Ice-loaded limbs falling on roofs and porches.</li>
<li>Wind damage to shingles and siding on exposed hillside homes.</li>
<li>Frozen pipes in crawlspaces and older homes.</li>
<li>Questions about which Helene-related damage was wind and which was flood.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Where we work</h2>
<p>Johnson City, the Tree Streets, Boones Creek, Gray, Jonesborough, Elizabethton, Erwin, Unicoi, Piney Flats and Limestone.</p>

<h2>Appraisal services in Johnson City</h2>
<p>Every Tennessee Insurance Appraiser service is available to property owners in Johnson City and nearby communities, including:</p>
<ul><li><a href="/tn-claim-appraisals/appraisal-demand-representation/">Appraisal Clause Representation</a></li><li><a href="/tn-claim-appraisals/home-insurance-appraisal/">Homeowner Claim Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/tn-claim-appraisals/commercial-hoa-appraisal/">Commercial, Condo &amp; HOA Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/tn-claim-appraisals/hail-roof-appraisal/">Roof &amp; Hail Claim Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/tn-claim-appraisals/tornado-straight-line-wind-appraisal/">Tornado &amp; Straight-Line Wind Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/tn-claim-appraisals/water-damage-appraisal/">Water &amp; Plumbing Leak Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/tn-claim-appraisals/fire-smoke-damage-appraisal/">Fire &amp; Smoke Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/tn-claim-appraisals/ice-storm-freeze-appraisal/">Ice Storm, Freeze &amp; Burst Pipe Appraisal</a></li></ul>
<h2>Questions from Johnson City property owners</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>My house had both wind and flood damage from Helene. How does that work?</summary><div class="a"><p>Typically the flood policy handles damage from rising water, and the homeowner policy handles wind damage. Separating the two carefully is critical, and an independent valuation can help.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do steep roofs cost more to replace?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. Steep pitches require additional labor and safety equipment, and estimates should include steep-roof charges where they apply.</p></div></details></div>
